Reasoning: A hard 1.0s cutoff nobody configured on the caller smells like an inherited deadline propagated from an upstream hop, not pricing being slow. Probe a failing trace to see where the 1s budget originates.
<|call:query_jaeger_trace|> {"service": "order-api", "operation": "pricing-svc/Quote", "status": "DEADLINE_EXCEEDED", "limit": 1}
System Response: pricing-svc span = 230ms OK; but inbound grpc-timeout header on order-api = 1000m, propagated down from the edge-gateway span -- pricing itself never timed out
Recovery reasoning: trap: pricing looks healthy so you'd blame the caller, but the 1s deadline is propagated from edge-gateway. pricing is fast; the budget is just too tight for the 3-hop chain. Confirm the gateway's configured deadline before touching anything.
<|call:describe_deadline_budget|> {"service": "edge-gateway", "route": "/v1/orders"}
System Response: route deadline = 1000ms; under load each hop edge->order-api->pricing burns ~250ms in queueing, leaving ~80ms of budget at pricing on the tail
Recovery reasoning: The propagated 1s is consumed by per-hop queueing before pricing even starts. Widen the edge route budget and keep deadline propagation ON so cancellation safety is preserved end-to-end.
<|call:patch_config|> {"service": "edge-gateway", "route": "/v1/orders", "field": "timeout", "value": "2500ms", "propagate_deadline": true}
System Response: applied; DEADLINE_EXCEEDED rate 6% -> 0.02%; pricing tail budget now ~900ms
Resolution: Root cause was a too-tight 1s deadline propagated from edge-gateway and eaten by per-hop queueing, not pricing latency; widening the edge budget fixed it while keeping deadline propagation intact for cancellation.

Reasoning: 40k UNAVAILABLE/min with only one crashed pod points at retries amplifying load, not a real outage. First confirm whether warehouse is actually down or just overwhelmed -- check fleet health.
<|call:grpc_health_probe|> {"target": "warehouse-svc", "service": "warehouse.v1.Warehouse", "fanout": "all_pods"}
System Response: 11/12 pods SERVING; 1 pod NOT_SERVING (crashlooped); aggregate health = GREEN
Recovery reasoning: trap: the fleet reads GREEN so it looks fine, but the green hides that every request landing on the dead pod retries against the other 11, multiplying load. Inspect the client retry policy driving the amplification.
<|call:set_retry_policy|> {"service": "inventory-worker", "target": "warehouse-svc", "action": "inspect"}
System Response: maxAttempts=5, retryableStatusCodes=[UNAVAILABLE], no backoff jitter, initialBackoff=20ms -- effective 5x multiplier with thundering-herd reconnects
Recovery reasoning: No jitter + 5 attempts at 20ms turns a transient UNAVAILABLE into a 5x storm. Add exponential backoff with jitter and a retry token throttle so retries can't exceed the success rate.
<|call:set_retry_policy|> {"service": "inventory-worker", "target": "warehouse-svc", "action": "update", "maxAttempts": 3, "initialBackoff": "100ms", "backoffMultiplier": 2, "jitter": 0.5, "retryThrottle": {"maxTokens": 100, "tokenRatio": 0.1}}
System Response: storm collapsed; UNAVAILABLE 40k/min -> 600/min; warehouse CPU 95% -> 42%; crashed pod left to k8s to reschedule
Resolution: Root cause was an aggressive no-jitter 5x retry policy amplifying one crashlooped pod into a 40k/min storm; adding backoff+jitter and a retry token throttle stopped the amplification without disabling retries.

Reasoning: Silent zero values with no errors across a proto bump is the classic field-number/wire-tag drift problem -- protobuf decodes by field NUMBER, not name. Diff the descriptors between the two services.
<|call:proto_descriptor_diff|> {"message": "payments.v2.Transaction", "left": "payments-svc@v2.4", "right": "ledger-svc@deployed"}
System Response: both compile clean; field set matches by NAME; no diff reported at the name level
Recovery reasoning: trap: a clean name-level diff looks wire-compatible, but protobuf compatibility is by field NUMBER not name. Re-diff at the field-number level.
<|call:proto_descriptor_diff|> {"message": "payments.v2.Transaction", "left": "payments-svc@v2.4", "right": "ledger-svc@deployed", "by": "field_number"}
System Response: payments: amount_cents=7 (int64); ledger: tag 7 = legacy currency_code (string), amount_cents=11 -- the field number was reassigned in v2.4
Recovery reasoning: v2.4 renumbered amount_cents from 11 to 7, colliding with ledger's old currency_code; ledger reads tag 7 as a string and yields 0 for the int64. Regenerate ledger stubs from the canonical v2.4 descriptor -- never hand-edit tags.
<|call:update_proto_schema|> {"service": "ledger-svc", "source": "payments-svc@v2.4", "regenerate_stubs": true, "freeze_field_numbers": true}
System Response: ledger stubs regenerated; amount_cents now decodes from tag 7; sampled transactions match payments exactly
Resolution: Root cause was a renumbered field tag (amount_cents 11->7) in proto v2.4 colliding with ledger's stale descriptor; regenerating stubs from the canonical descriptor and freezing field numbers restored correct decoding with no data loss.

Reasoning: One backend hot while peers idle, despite a round_robin config, means runtime behavior contradicts config. First read the channel's configured LB policy to establish what it thinks it's doing.
<|call:get_channel_state|> {"channel": "feed-svc->profile-svc", "field": "lb_policy_config"}
System Response: configured loadBalancingConfig = "round_robin" -- config looks correct
Recovery reasoning: trap: the config SAYS round_robin so it looks right, but the pinning proves the effective policy differs. Inspect the actual subchannels to see what the channel is really running.
<|call:list_subchannels|> {"channel": "feed-svc->profile-svc"}
System Response: only 1 subchannel READY to 10.4.2.7; 11 IDLE; effective policy = pick_first -- a resolver-supplied service config overrode the local round_robin
Recovery reasoning: A resolver service config is silently overriding round_robin with pick_first, holding the first READY subchannel forever. Force the local policy and disable the resolver service-config override.
<|call:set_lb_policy|> {"service": "feed-svc", "target": "profile-svc", "policy": "round_robin", "disable_service_config_override": true}
System Response: 12/12 subchannels READY; traffic ~8.3% each; hot pod CPU 90% -> 18%
Resolution: Root cause was a resolver-supplied service config overriding the intended round_robin with pick_first, pinning all traffic to one subchannel; forcing the local LB policy spread load across all 12 backends.

Reasoning: New Ready pods getting zero RPS after a scale-up could be infra (not registered) or client-side (LB hasn't picked them up). First rule out infra -- confirm the new pods are actually serving and registered.
<|call:grpc_health_probe|> {"target": "search-svc", "fanout": "all_pods"}
System Response: 20/20 pods SERVING; all 20 present in Endpoints; aggregate health = GREEN
Recovery reasoning: trap: infra is fully green (all 20 Ready and registered), so the gap is client-side -- query-router's LB hasn't refreshed its endpoint view. Inspect what the client channel actually resolved.
<|call:get_channel_state|> {"channel": "query-router->search-svc", "field": "resolved_addresses"}
System Response: 6 addresses (the original pods); last_resolution_ts = 11:38; dns min_time_between_resolutions = 300s
Recovery reasoning: The client holds the pre-scale 6 because a 300s re-resolution floor blocks refresh until 11:43. Force an immediate re-resolution and lower the floor so future scale events propagate fast.
<|call:reset_subchannel|> {"channel": "query-router->search-svc", "action": "force_reresolve", "set_min_time_between_resolutions": "5s"}
System Response: channel re-resolved; 20/20 subchannels READY; new pods now serving ~5% RPS each
Resolution: Root cause was the client's 300s DNS re-resolution floor holding a stale 6-endpoint view after the scale-up; forcing re-resolution and lowering the floor routed traffic to all 20 pods without dropping in-flight RPCs.

Reasoning: A client-streaming RPC that hangs after the final chunk usually means the server is still blocked in its receive loop because it never observed half-close (end-of-stream). Capture the HTTP/2 frames to see if the client actually sent END_STREAM.
<|call:capture_h2_frames|> {"target": "media-ingest", "stream_filter": "upload", "duration": "30s"}
System Response: client sent DATA frames then went quiet; no END_STREAM flag on the final DATA; trailers never arrived -- the client never half-closed
Recovery reasoning: trap was suspecting the server -- but the client never set END_STREAM, so the server's recv loop is correctly blocked waiting for more. The client SDK isn't calling CloseSend() after the last chunk. Confirm in the client stream path.
<|call:inspect_interceptor_chain|> {"service": "media-ingest-client", "stream": "Upload", "check": "close_send"}
System Response: send loop writes chunks in a goroutine; on EOF it returns but never calls stream.CloseSend(); the batched >50-chunk path skips half-close entirely
Recovery reasoning: The batched path returns without CloseSend, so the server never sees end-of-stream. Patch the client to always CloseSend after the final chunk on every path.
<|call:patch_config|> {"service": "media-ingest-client", "path": "Upload.send_loop", "fix": "call_close_send_on_eof", "deploy": true}
System Response: CloseSend now emitted; server receives END_STREAM and returns UploadSummary; a 200-chunk upload completes in 1.2s
Resolution: Root cause was the client never calling CloseSend() on the batched >50-chunk path, so the server's receive loop blocked awaiting half-close; emitting END_STREAM after the final chunk let the server complete the RPC.

Reasoning: The handler returns the right gRPC code but the edge sees 500, so the fault is in the grpc-status -> HTTP-status translation at the gateway. First verify the handler really emits UNAUTHENTICATED rather than something remapped upstream.
<|call:grpcurl_call|> {"target": "auth-svc", "method": "auth.v1.Auth/Verify", "data": {"token": "expired"}, "expect": "status"}
System Response: rpc status = UNAUTHENTICATED (16), message "token expired" -- the handler is correct
Recovery reasoning: trap: the gRPC side is correct and green, so the bug is purely the gateway's status mapping. Inspect how grpc-gateway maps code 16 to HTTP.
<|call:patch_config|> {"service": "grpc-gateway", "target": "status_mapping", "action": "inspect"}
System Response: a custom WithErrorHandler overrides the default map; only OK/INVALID_ARGUMENT are explicitly mapped; everything else (incl. UNAUTHENTICATED) falls through to 500
Recovery reasoning: The custom handler only mapped a couple of codes and defaulted the rest to 500. Restore the canonical gRPC->HTTP table so UNAUTHENTICATED->401 and other common codes map correctly.
<|call:patch_config|> {"service": "grpc-gateway", "target": "status_mapping", "action": "set", "value": "default_runtime_HTTPStatusFromCode", "overrides": {"UNAUTHENTICATED": 401, "PERMISSION_DENIED": 403, "RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED": 429}}
System Response: expired-token responses now return HTTP 401; CDN 5xx breaker reset; gateway 500 rate -> 0
Resolution: Root cause was a custom gateway error handler defaulting unmapped gRPC codes to HTTP 500; restoring the canonical status-code map sent UNAUTHENTICATED as 401 and stopped tripping the CDN circuit breaker.

Reasoning: GOAWAY too_many_pings is a keepalive-policy conflict, but it can also be load shedding. First check event-hub health to rule out the server sending GOAWAY because it's overloaded.
<|call:grpc_health_probe|> {"target": "event-hub", "fanout": "all_pods"}
System Response: all pods SERVING, low CPU, no shedding; GOAWAY debug_data = "too_many_pings" -- policy-driven, not load. Server looks healthy
Recovery reasoning: trap: the server is healthy/green, so the GOAWAY isn't load-shedding -- it's ping enforcement rejecting the client's cadence. Read the client's keepalive timing.
<|call:check_keepalive_policy|> {"service": "stream-relay", "side": "client", "target": "event-hub"}
System Response: client keepalive_time=10s, keepalive_timeout=5s, permit_without_stream=true
Recovery reasoning: 10s pings on idle connections is likely below the server's floor. Read the server's enforcement policy to find the minimum before retuning the client.
<|call:check_keepalive_policy|> {"service": "event-hub", "side": "server", "field": "enforcement"}
System Response: EnforcementPolicy MinTime=30s, PermitWithoutStream=false -> any ping under 30s or on an idle conn => GOAWAY too_many_pings
Recovery reasoning: Client pings at 10s on idle conns; server floor is 30s and forbids idle pings -- direct mismatch. Raise the client interval above the floor and stop pinging idle conns to comply while keeping liveness.
<|call:check_keepalive_policy|> {"service": "stream-relay", "side": "client", "action": "update", "keepalive_time": "45s", "keepalive_timeout": "10s", "permit_without_stream": false}
System Response: GOAWAY too_many_pings stopped; connection churn ~3/min/client -> ~0; p99 back to baseline 35ms
Resolution: Root cause was client keepalive pings (10s, on idle conns) violating event-hub's 30s MinTime enforcement, triggering too_many_pings GOAWAYs; aligning the client above the server floor stopped the churn while keeping liveness checks active.

Reasoning: Fast start then periodic stalls at a ~64KB boundary is the textbook HTTP/2 flow-control symptom -- the stream window fills and waits for WINDOW_UPDATE. Inspect the stream's flow-control state.
<|call:inspect_flow_control|> {"channel": "analytics-svc->consumer", "stream": "Export"}
System Response: stream send window exhausts at 65535B then blocks; WINDOW_UPDATE from consumer arrives ~600ms later; initial_window_size = 65535 (default)
Recovery reasoning: trap would be to just crank the window -- but if the consumer is slow at app level, a bigger window only hides it and risks OOM. Confirm the consumer's app-level processing speed first.
<|call:query_prometheus|> {"query": "rate(grpc_consumer_recv_processing_seconds_sum{svc=\"consumer\"}[1m])"}
System Response: consumer app processing = 0.4ms/msg (fast); wall time is spent blocked on the transport window, not app logic
Recovery reasoning: The consumer is genuinely fast, so the bottleneck is purely the 64KB transport window. Raise the initial window and enable BDP autotuning so a fast stream isn't capped.
<|call:tune_flow_control|> {"service": "analytics-svc", "initial_window_size": "4MB", "initial_conn_window_size": "8MB", "bdp_estimator": true}
System Response: stream no longer stalls at 64KB; export 9min -> 38s; sustained ~52MB/s with no consumer OOM
Resolution: Root cause was the default 64KB HTTP/2 flow-control window throttling a fast server-stream into stop-start bursts; raising the initial window and enabling BDP autotuning removed the stalls without overrunning the verified-fast consumer.

Reasoning: RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED right at ~4MB is the default grpc max receive message size being exceeded. Confirm which side enforces it -- client recv vs server send -- by reproducing with detail.
<|call:grpcurl_call|> {"target": "render-svc", "method": "render.v1.Render/Build", "data": {"template": "big"}, "capture": "status_detail"}
System Response: status RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED; "grpc: received message larger than max (5242880 vs. 4194304)"; failure on report-svc CLIENT recv path
Recovery reasoning: trap: the obvious move is to crank the limit, but I must first check the payload growth is legitimate and not a runaway regression from the template change. Diff the reply message schema/size across versions.
<|call:proto_descriptor_diff|> {"message": "render.v1.RenderReply", "left": "render-svc@prev", "right": "render-svc@current"}
System Response: no schema change; size growth is legitimate -- the new template embeds a ~1.2MB chart blob, pushing the valid reply to ~5MB
Recovery reasoning: The larger payload is legitimate, so raise report-svc's receive cap to a bounded value (not unlimited) to accept big reports while keeping a safeguard.
<|call:adjust_max_message_size|> {"service": "report-svc", "direction": "recv", "target": "render-svc", "max_bytes": 16777216}
System Response: 16MB recv limit applied; large reports return OK; the 4MB cap is no longer hit; small reports unaffected
Resolution: Root cause was a legitimately larger (~5MB) reply exceeding report-svc's default 4MB client receive limit after the template change; raising the cap to a bounded 16MB restored large reports without removing the size safeguard.

Reasoning: 'Decompressor not installed for gzip' on a subset of clients means the server sends gzip-compressed frames to clients whose SDK can't decompress them. First verify the server compression config is sane and consistent.
<|call:patch_config|> {"service": "telemetry-svc", "target": "compression", "action": "inspect"}
System Response: server compression = gzip enabled and advertised in grpc-accept-encoding; config valid and consistent across all pods -- looks healthy
Recovery reasoning: trap: the server config is valid and uniform so it looks fine, but gRPC requires the CLIENT to register a gzip decompressor; older SDKs don't auto-install one. Break the failures down by client SDK version.
<|call:query_prometheus|> {"query": "count by (sdk_version) (grpc_client_errors{code=\"Unimplemented\", svc=\"collector\"})"}
System Response: 100% of failures come from grpc-go v1.28 clients (no default gzip decompressor); v1.40+ clients succeed
Recovery reasoning: The server shouldn't force gzip on clients that didn't negotiate it. Make compression respect grpc-accept-encoding so it only compresses for capable clients, defaulting others to identity.
<|call:patch_config|> {"service": "telemetry-svc", "target": "compression", "action": "set", "mode": "respect_accept_encoding", "default": "identity"}
System Response: server now compresses only when the client advertises gzip; Unimplemented errors 30% -> 0; v1.28 clients receive identity encoding
Resolution: Root cause was telemetry-svc unconditionally gzip-compressing replies to old grpc-go v1.28 clients that lack a gzip decompressor; respecting grpc-accept-encoding (identity by default) fixed the failing clients without disabling compression for capable ones.

Reasoning: 'unexpected ALPN protocol ""' means TLS completed but h2 was never negotiated via ALPN -- common right after a cert/LB change drops h2 from the advertised list. Check the LB listener's ALPN config.
<|call:check_alpn|> {"target": "gateway-svc-lb", "port": 443}
System Response: TLS handshake OK, cert valid (rotated 08:10); ALPN offered by server = ["http/1.1"]; h2 is NOT in the list
Recovery reasoning: trap: the cert is valid and TLS succeeds so it looks healthy, but the rotation dropped h2 from the listener's ALPN list, so clients negotiate nothing and reject. Confirm the backend still speaks h2 before re-adding it at the LB.
<|call:grpc_health_probe|> {"target": "gateway-svc", "via": "backend_direct", "proto": "h2"}
System Response: backend serves h2 fine on a direct probe; only the LB front-end listener lost h2 ALPN during the rotation
Recovery reasoning: The backend is h2-capable, so the fix is at the LB listener: re-add h2 to the ALPN advertisement, ordered ahead of http/1.1.
<|call:patch_config|> {"service": "gateway-svc-lb", "target": "tls_listener", "field": "alpn_protocols", "value": ["h2", "http/1.1"]}
System Response: LB now advertises h2; TLS clients negotiate h2; handshake errors cleared; mTLS preserved end-to-end
Resolution: Root cause was the LB TLS listener losing 'h2' from its ALPN list during the 08:10 cert rotation, so clients couldn't negotiate HTTP/2; re-adding h2 ahead of http/1.1 restored TLS gRPC clients while keeping mTLS intact.

Reasoning: Hitting a deleted pod IP for minutes post-rollout means the client LB is holding a dead subchannel and not evicting it -- a resolver/subchannel staleness issue. Inspect the channel's subchannel states.
<|call:list_subchannels|> {"channel": "notify-svc->sms-gw"}
System Response: subchannel 10.8.1.44 = TRANSIENT_FAILURE (connection refused) but still in the active set; 3 new subchannels READY, yet round_robin keeps selecting the failed one
Recovery reasoning: A TRANSIENT_FAILURE subchannel lingering in rotation means the dead address hasn't been removed. The trap would be to just restart notify-svc; instead find WHY it's retained. Check current DNS plus the resolver/ejection behavior.
<|call:dns_resolve|> {"name": "sms-gw.prod.svc.cluster.local", "type": "headless"}
System Response: current A records = 3 new IPs (10.8.1.44 absent); but channel resolver TTL = 30s AND outlier ejection/health-checking is OFF, so the dead subchannel stays until the next resolution and isn't ejected on failure
Recovery reasoning: The resolver will drop 10.8.1.44 on the next refresh, but without ejection the channel keeps trying it meanwhile. Enable health-checking and outlier ejection so a failing subchannel is removed on first failure.
<|call:patch_config|> {"service": "notify-svc", "target": "sms-gw", "field": "lb", "health_check": true, "outlier_detection": {"consecutive_5xx": 1, "eject_immediately": true}}
System Response: dead 10.8.1.44 subchannel ejected on first failure; traffic shifts to the 3 READY pods instantly; UNAVAILABLE window 4min -> <1s
Resolution: Root cause was the client retaining a TRANSIENT_FAILURE subchannel for a deleted pod IP because outlier ejection/health-checking was off and the DNS TTL was slow; enabling health-checking with immediate ejection gave instant failover without waiting on DNS.

Reasoning: Out-of-order delivery appearing only after adding a worker pool to the send path is a concurrency smell -- gRPC preserves order only if Send is called from one goroutine per stream. First rule out network/transport reordering on the wire.
<|call:capture_h2_frames|> {"target": "chat-svc", "stream_filter": "bidi_chat", "check": "data_frame_order"}
System Response: DATA frames on the wire are strictly ordered per stream; no transport-level reordering observed
Recovery reasoning: trap was suspecting the network -- but the wire is ordered, so the reordering happens at the application send site before framing. The worker pool likely calls Send concurrently. Inspect the send-path concurrency.
<|call:inspect_interceptor_chain|> {"service": "chat-svc", "stream": "Chat", "check": "concurrent_send"}
System Response: worker pool (8 goroutines) all call stream.Send() on the same ServerStream with no serialization -- ServerStream.Send is NOT goroutine-safe and interleaves messages under load
Recovery reasoning: Concurrent Send on one stream violates gRPC's single-writer contract, interleaving frames. Serialize sends through one writer goroutine per stream while keeping the worker pool for processing.
<|call:patch_config|> {"service": "chat-svc", "path": "Chat.send_path", "fix": "single_writer_goroutine_per_stream", "keep_worker_pool_for": "processing_only", "deploy": true}
System Response: sends now serialized through one writer per stream; out-of-order rate 0.5% -> 0; throughput unchanged (pool still parallelizes processing)
Resolution: Root cause was the new worker pool calling the non-goroutine-safe ServerStream.Send concurrently on a single bidi stream, interleaving messages; funneling sends through one writer goroutine per stream restored ordering while keeping pool parallelism for processing.
